#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2524 :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"}]}
